Theorem 1 (inverse map T = L, I = U - L, F = 1 - U): the normalised triple that the classical
frame actually carries for each course, i.e. the natural extension on {pass, fail}:
  DE: stated (0.5, 0.1, 0.2) -> carried (0.5, 0.3, 0.2)
  SA: stated (0.6, 0.2, 0.4) -> carried (0.6, 0.0, 0.4)
  FM: stated (0.8, 0.0, 0.1) -> carried (0.8, 0.1, 0.1)
  SM: stated (0.4, 0.3, 0.5) -> carried (0.4, 0.1, 0.5)

Intuitionistic and neutrosophic forms give the same intervals (Theorem 4(b): kernel = I direction):
  DE: neutrosophic (0.5, 0.8)  intuitionistic (0.5, 0.8)  equal = True
  SA: neutrosophic (0.6, 0.6)  intuitionistic (0.6, 0.6)  equal = True
  FM: neutrosophic (0.8, 0.9)  intuitionistic (0.8, 0.9)  equal = True
  SM: neutrosophic (0.4, 0.5)  intuitionistic (0.4, 0.5)  equal = True
